## Question 6 

Compare age at the time of nonprofit formation for male and female entrepreneurs. 

* Variable Name: **age** 
* Variable Type: **numeric** 
* Survey question: *What was your age when you created the nonprofit?*

```{r}
dat%>%
  group_by(gender)%>%
 summarize(min.age = min(age,na.rm=T),
      median.age = median(age,na.rm=T),
      mean.age = mean(age,na.rm=T),
      max.age = max(age,na.rm=T)) %>%
pander()
                      

ggplot(dat,aes(x=age, fill=gender))+
    geom_density(alpha=0.5)
xlab("Age")
  labs(x="Gender",y="Age at Founding",title="Age at Nonprofit formation by Gender")
  
  
```
```{r}

t.test(age~gender,data=dat)

```

***ANSWER***

Men were older at founding on average with an average age of 55.0 years old and a SD=12.9, than women at 51.9 years old with a SD=11.6. This difference is ***statistically significant*** 

* ***t=3.17***
* ***p=0.0016***

We reject the null hypothesis. Men founded their non profits approximately three years later in life, on average, than women. 


## Question 7 

Compare income levels prior to starting the nonprofit for male and female entrepreneurs. 

* Variable Name: **income** 
* Variable Type: **numeric** 
* Survey question: *Please specify your income range prior to working to create this nonprofit:*

```{r}
dat%>%
  group_by(gender)%>%
  summarize(min.income=min(income, na.rm=T),
            median.income=median(income,na.rm=T),
            mean.income=mean(income, na.rm=T),
            max.income=max(income,na.rm=T)) %>%
  
  pander()

ggplot(dat,aes(x=income, fill=gender))+
         geom_density(alpha=0.5)+
  xlab("Income")
         labs(x="Gender",y="Pre-Founding Income", title="Income Prior to Founding by Gender")
       
```
```{r}
t.test(income~gender, data=dat)
```

***ANSWER***

Men reported a substatially higher pre-founding income, $80.0883 average, SD=$45,939, than women at $67,742 average, SD=$46,266. This is statistically significant.

* ***t=3.64***
* ***p=0.0003***

We reject the null hypothesis. Men in entrepreneurship had significantly higher income than females prior to founding. 

## Question 8

Based upon these seven contrasts, would you conclude that the resources male and female nonprofit entrepreneurs have at the time of founding were equivalent?

No. While five of the seven resources show no meaningfullness in gender differences, the two that do play a significant role. 

* ***Age at Founding*** : Men were on average 3.1 years older than women at the time if founding. (p=0.0016)
* ***Pre-Founding Income***: Men Averaged $13,141 in additional income over women prior to founding.  (p=0.0003)

These factors are ideal to give one group a head start independent of anything about the venture itself. Someone who is older, likely has additional savings, more professional networks available, and more availability to absorb early losses. 

**Q8-A:**

What is the adjusted decision criteria used for contrasts to maintain an alpha of 0.05 for the omnibus test of group equivalence? 

With seven contrasts and a desired family wise α of 0.05, the Bonferroni adjust critical value per test is:

$$\alpha_{adjusted} = \frac{0.05}{7} = 0.00714$$

Any individual contrast needs p<0.00714 to be declared significnat while controlling the over all false-positive rate across all seven tests. 

**Q8-B:**

What is the lowest p-value you observed across the seven contrasts? 

The lowest observed p-value across all seven tests is p=0.0003 in regards to income. 

**Q8-C:**

Can we claim study group equivalency? Why or why not? 

Group equivalence cannot be claimed. Of the seven contrasts, five remain insignificant to gender differences in regards to education, eperience, seed funding, debt, and funding source, however, two survive even with Bonferroni correction. Age with a p=0.0016 and income with a p=0.0003 both significantly below the 0.00714 threshold. This information means that male and female entrepreneurs entering a nonprofit founding with meaninfully difference personal resources, men were older and had higher pre-founding income. Since these resources, financial capital and career-stage capital, are linked to affecting seed funding, debt capacity, and organizational survival, this becomes a threat to treating male and female founders as pre-existing equivalent groups in any downstream comparison, since the baseline isn't balanced on these dimesions even after adjusting and with multiple comparisons. 

